landing-review: vulkan-only plan, landing 11-5e-2 -- the GL restorer goes whole

Range reviewed: main...HEAD (6ff81e2..3f28f47, 4 commits: 15e9148 code, 3ccf8c3
docs, 1abc0f1 the review's fixes, 3f28f47 the re-measurement).
The review read 6ff81e2..3ccf8c3; 1abc0f1 and 3f28f47 are its result.
Reviewer: two dedicated read-only agents, `high`.
WHY `high` AND NOT `medium`: the landing is a deletion, but as first written it
made one previously-dormant data path LIVE. `tagpu_gaf.c`'s `atlas_paint` ended
in `if (a->job) restore_enqueue(a, e);` -- a GL predicate gating the feed of the
PUBLISHED FRAME LIST, which is the Vulkan restorer's input. Removing that guard
means `a->rlist` grows on every atlas paint while three producers publish its
POINTER to the Vulkan passes: the lifetime of something one side writes and
another reads, which is CLAUDE.md's `high` clause. Reviewed as one rather than
argued down to medium -- AND THAT IS THE CLAUSE THAT PAID.

WHAT IT DOES. Deletes `tagpu_restoreglsl.c` entire -- 39 functions, 249 of the
tree's 540 masked GL call sites, the GLSL backend of the Classic++ restorer --
with the `tagpu_gaf.c` code that fed it, the three blocks that stepped it (two
in `tagpu_native.c`, one in `tagpu_gui_surf.c`) and one dead accessor. Surface
540 -> 252; files with a GL call six -> four. 47 functions gone, inventory
diffed per TU with nothing else taken, every deleted non-static absent from
every object file, `tagpu_rglsl_tileable` (which lives in `tagpu_restore_core.c`)
still linking.

THE FIND: COROLLARY 2 CAUGHT IN THE ACT. The root predicate is `a->tex`, and
landing 11-4c set it to 0 on purpose the same day, for a reason it stated
correctly -- a re-created atlas must not carry a stale texture name -- without
noticing that the same line put 288 GL call sites and the Vulkan lane's restore
feed out of reach. The missing `LoadLibrary` (nothing calls `oglu_load_dll`) is
a second pin underneath and would stop the same tree alone, but it is NOT the
root, and naming it as the root would have sent the next reader to the wrong
file.

AND UNPINNING IT RAN A CONSUMER THAT HAD NEVER RUN -- corollary 1, measured in
process with a temporary probe:
  pre-landing:  PROBE feat: PAINT AFTER ARM n=15 job=NULL tex=0 rlistN=0
                      (this paint will NOT enqueue)
                vk: feat: restoring the atlas HERE - 0 of 0 frames ; 0 drains
  post-fix:     PROBE2 fx: paint rlistWant=1 rlist=SET rlistN=0 -> 1
                vk: feat: 15 of 15 ; vk: fx: 5 of 5 ; 162 queue drains

=== THE HIGH: THAT ONE-LINE FIX IS A USE-AFTER-FREE, AND IT IS OUT ===

Reviewer 2 found it; reviewer 1 did not, and the two only appear to disagree --
reviewer 1 cleared `feat`/`fx`, reviewer 2 agrees those are safe by ordering and
says the UNIT atlas is not. Every link was then checked against the source
before anything was touched:

  the published pointer is RAW   tagpu_render3do.c tagpu_r3d_atlas_restore_list
                                 ends `return s_atlas.rlist;`
  captured at publish            tagpu_posedraw.c pd_view_publish:
                                 `s_pub.restoreFrames = fr;`
  on the FIRST window of a frame its one caller, pd_begin:
                                 `int first = (s_win++ == 0); ... if (first && s_recording)`
  ghost paints AFTER that        tagpu_native.c: tagpu_posedraw_begin(&s_pv) ...
                                 then ghost_record(...), whose comment reads
                                 "in its own window after the units' ...
                                  It is AFTER on purpose"
  and a ghost reaches the paint  tagpu_r3d_atlas_uv -> atlas_get ->
                                 tagpu_gaf_atlas_get, which paints on a miss
  where the buffer moves         restore_enqueue -> rlist_add -> rlist_room ->
                                 realloc (or, out of memory, free)

So on any frame where a build ghost needs a texture not yet atlased, the unit
atlas's list can be reallocated AFTER its address was published, and the render
thread dereferences the old one in the same iteration. Feature and effects
escape only because their publication happens to be the last write of their
frame; the unit atlas does not, and nothing enforces it.

THE DECISION: the feed is NOT restored by this landing. Not calling
`restore_enqueue` is exactly today's behaviour -- `a->job` has been NULL for the
life of the process -- so the deletion stays behaviour-preserving and the defect
is documented rather than half-fixed. A by-design fix exists and is written down
(a BOUND: allocate `rlist_cap(a)` once in the arm and make `rlist_room` a pure
bounds test, 0.36 MB unit / 0.72 MB feat against a mirror this file already
declines to free at 16 MB; plus an ORDERING for `rlist_restart`, which rewrites
in place and so is not covered by the bound: take the unit list in
`tagpu_posedraw_handover`). Opening a live data path is a different landing from
deleting a backend, with its own measurement and its own review: filed as
11-5e-2c in the plan, the roadmap and at the site in `atlas_paint`.

AND THE DELETION LEAVES THE PROPERTY STRONGER THAN IT FOUND IT. `restore_enqueue`
and `rlist_add` were callerless once the backend went, so both are deleted too --
and with them the only path by which a published buffer could move. `rlist_room`
is now reachable only from `rlist_restart`, and `rlist_restart` only from
`tagpu_gaf_atlas_restore_vk`, which latches on `a->rlistWant` and runs once per
atlas per session, before anything has published a non-NULL pointer. So
`a->rlist` is assigned exactly once and is neither moved nor freed for the life
of the atlas: a LIFETIME the code enforces, where before it rested on `a->job`
being NULL for a reason stated three files away -- which is precisely what THE
NAMED RULE is about.

TWO MORE PINS THAT 11-5e-2c MUST CLEAR (reviewer 1's M1 and M2, both verified):
`restored` is published by THREE producers, not one, and all three are gated on
`s_atlas.rgb`, whose only remaining write in the tree is `a->rgb = 0` in
`tagpu_gaf_atlas_lost` -- so feeding the list is not sufficient to make the
restore visible; the consumers take their "no restore" arm first. And `rgbAniso`
lost its only writer here, so it reports 0 while `tagpu_vk_unit.c` stands the
whole frame down on `h.atlasRgbAniso != s_twinAniso`, where `s_twinAniso` is
`tagpu_classicpp_light()->aniso`, DEFAULT 4. Unreachable only while `restored`
is 0. Unpin one without the other and the unit pass draws nothing. All labelled
[PINNED] at their declarations in `tagpu_gaf.h`.

NO PIXEL A/B COULD HAVE CAUGHT THE FEED DEFECT. 11-4c's gate was 0 px over 64
cross-build pairs and was honestly run; the feed exists only under
`tagpu_restorevk.on`, which that fixture did not arm. 11-5e-1's HIGH
generalised: A PIXEL DIFF CANNOT COVER A LEVER THE FIXTURE DOES NOT ARM.

=== VERIFIED BY RUNNING IT ===

Re-run after the fix, on a fresh instance `e5a`, `one-unit`, Two Continents,
1024x768, renderer=vulkan, a private display. THREE full runs, each settled to a
steady `vk: census: ... 2 pass(es) drew (terr=1 unit=1)`:

  within one run, three grabs, all three runs (nine pairs):  0 px  -- STATIC
  the SAME build, two separate RUNS (the noise floor):      44 px  -- all minimap
  main's build vs this build:                               44 px  -- 0 OUTSIDE

The cross-build figure is the noise floor TO THE PIXEL, and not one pixel outside
the minimap rect differs. The minimap is where both figures live: its blobs are
greyscale in one run and player-coloured in another, at different positions, so it
varies run to run on its own. Three grabs inside one run prove the scene is static
and bound NOTHING about run-to-run variation -- a different question, a different
control. 11-5e-1 reported "44 px, 0 outside" without that control and so had no
bound on its own noise.

AND THE LOG IS THE DIRECT EVIDENCE, because the claim is behaviour-preservation.
The lines that could have changed are byte-identical between main's ddraw.dll and
this one:
    fx:   restorevk -- ... (0 entries seeded, 8192-frame bound)
    feat: restorevk -- ... (0 entries seeded, 16384-frame bound)
    unit: restorevk -- ... (25 entries seeded, 8192-frame bound)
    vk: unit: restoring the twin HERE - 25 of 25 frames over 2048x2048, generation 1
That is "the feed stays shut" at run time: seeded once, never fed, exactly as before.

THE FIXTURE COST MORE THAN THE MEASUREMENT, and the cause is a tacli defect worth
naming. Three runs failed first, all identically: our DLL loaded, wrote its ~18 hook
and patch lines, then stopped at window creation on TA's modal "Error: Environment
Initialization Failed! Check your DirectX setup", no ErrorLog.txt, neither rendering
nor exiting. IT REPRODUCED WITH MAIN'S DLL, which is the tell that the build under
test is innocent. Cause: `tacli --display` is honoured only by `create` -- `_launch`
builds its env from `Instance.env()`, which reads `meta["display"]`, and nothing
persists the flag. The instances were pinned to a display a parallel session had left
behind and which had since died. Written into the ta-drive skill, with the two
adjacent traps: a hand-rolled `wine TotalA.exe` without WINEDLLOVERRIDES=ddraw=n,b
loads wine's builtin ddraw and fails the same way, proving nothing; and every instance
prefix shares ONE user.reg inode (300 hardlinks, template included), so registry
writes are global -- real, and NOT the cause here.

